  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry weather will continue as temperatures warm \nto seasonable values by Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to extend down from the north into \nthe region. Temperatures will finally warm back up to \nseasonable values on Wednesday with afternoon highs ranging in \nthe low 70s across the region. Breezy northeasterly winds will \ncontinue with speeds 10 to 15 mph with gusts up to 20 mph \n(highest along the coastline). We could see periods of showers \ndevelop within a nearby coastal trough, it's possible these \nshowers could shift onshore at times (esp. across the the \nsoutheast Georgia coastline). The best chance for precipitation \nwill be mid to late week timeframe. Regardless, no significant \nrainfall amounts are expected through Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An area of low pressure should develop off the \nSoutheast coast and bring much needed widespread rainfall to \nsoutheast Georgia and southeast South Carolina late this week \nand into this weekend. \n\nGlobal forecast models are trending towards a surface low \npressure system developing near the Atlantic coast of Florida \nand tracking up the Southeast coast. While the timing, \nproximity to the coast, and potential inland extent of any \nrainfall are details that will need to be ironed out at a later \nforecast update, there is increasing potential for a widespread \nrainfall event. Rainfall is much needed across southeastern SC \nand southeastern GA, with all three of our local climate sites \n(KCHS, Downtown Charleston, and KSAV) on track for the 2nd \ndriest years on record.",
